Mastra now supports the MCP 2026-07-28 specification with` @mastra/mcp` ^2.0.0. The new spec removes server sessions, so every request is self-contained. If you build[MCP servers](https://mastra.ai/docs/connections/mcp) with Mastra, you can now scale them like any other HTTP service, across load-balanced instances, or on serverless platforms that scale to zero.


## How MCP 1.0 worked


In the[previous MCP spec](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-11-25) , a client opened every connection with an` initialize` request. The server created a session, sent back a session ID, and every following request had to include it.


This pinned the client to whichever server instance issued the session. If you ran multiple servers behind a load balancer, a request could be routed to an instance that didn't recognize the session ID, resulting in` 400 session not found` errors.


The workarounds were sticky sessions or a shared session store like Redis. Both solutions worked, but they added complexity, latency, and potentially cost.


