## What changed in MCP 2.0


There are two key changes to the[2026-07-28 spec](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/changelog) :


- [SEP-2575](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/pull/2575) removes` initialize` handshake.
- [SEP-2567](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/pull/2567) removes` Mcp-Session-Id` header and protocol-level sessions.


Every request is now independent and behaves like a regular HTTP request. Any instance can respond to any request, so a traditional round-robin load balancer works without sticky sessions or a session store. If an instance crashes, the load balancer routes the next request to another instance and the client continues as normal.


The new spec also opens up more deployment options. With no session to keep alive, a server doesn't have to stay up between requests. On serverless platforms it can scale to zero when it's not in use, saving you money.


Your browser does not support the video tag.


### HTTP headers


Every HTTP request now includes an` Mcp-Method` header, plus an` Mcp-Name` header for tool calls, prompts, and resources, letting gateways, rate limiters, and firewalls make decisions about the request without needing to parse the request body:


HTTP request


```text
POST /api/mcp/mastra-blog/mcp HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
Mcp-Protocol-Version: 2026-07-28
Mcp-Method: tools/call
Mcp-Name: emailPost


{
"jsonrpc": "2.0",
"id": 3,
"method": "tools/call",
"params": {
"name": "emailPost",
"arguments": { "slug": "introducing-code-mode" }
}
}
```


### Cache hints


List results such as` tools/list` and` resources/list` now include two hints borrowed from HTTP caching:


- ` ttlMs` : How long the result stays fresh.
- ` cacheScope` : Whether the result can be shared across users (` public` ) or only reused for the caller (` private` ).


src/mastra/mcp/blog-server.ts


```text
export   const   blogServer   =   new   MCPServer  ({
// ...
cacheHints: {
"  tools/list  "  : { ttlMs:   60_000  , cacheScope:   "  public  "   },
"  resources/list  "  : { ttlMs:   30_000  , cacheScope:   "  private  "   }
}
});
```


### server/discover


The` server/discover` method helps you understand an MCP server's capabilities. In Mastra, use` getServerInfo()` to see the underlying details:


src/mastra/mcp/blog-client.ts


```text
import   {   MCPClient   }   from   "  @mastra/mcp  "  ;


export   const   blogClient   =   new   MCPClient  ({
servers: {
blog: { url:   new   URL  (  "  http://localhost:4111/api/mcp/mastra-blog/mcp  "  ) }
}
});


await   blogClient.  listTools  ();   // Connects to the server and runs server/discover


blogClient.  getServerInfo  ();   // Returns the server's name, version, and description
```


A response includes the supported MCP spec versions, its capabilities, and a name and description:


response


```text
{
"  jsonrpc  "  :   "  2.0  "  ,
"  id  "  :   1  ,
"  result  "  :   {
"  supportedVersions  "  :   [  "  2026-07-28  "  ],
"  capabilities  "  :   {   "  tools  "  :   {   "  listChanged  "  :   true   }   },
"  _meta  "  :   {
"  io.modelcontextprotocol/serverInfo  "  :   {
"  name  "  :   "  Mastra Blog  "  ,
"  version  "  :   "  1.0.0  "  ,
"  description  "  :   "  Search and read posts from the Mastra blog.  "
}
}
}
}
```


### requestState


The` requestState` lets a server ask the user a question mid-call without holding the connection open. In Mastra, use` suspend()` from a tool's` execute` function to ask the question and` resumeData` to read the answer:


src/mastra/tools/email-post.ts


```text
import   {   createTool   }   from   "  @mastra/core/tools  "  ;
import   {   z   }   from   "  zod  "  ;


export   const   emailPost   =   createTool  ({
// ...
resumeSchema: z.  object  ({ email: z.  string  () }),   // The answer you need, sent to the client as a form
execute  :   async   ({ slug }, context)   =>   {
if   (  !  context.resumeData) {
await   context.  suspend  ?.({ message:   "  What email address should I send the post to?  "   });   // Ends the call with the question and a requestState
return  ;
}
return   { sentTo: context.resumeData.email };   // resumeData holds the user's answer
}
});
```


A response includes the question and a` requestState` , a serialized string of the paused call:


response


```text
{
"  jsonrpc  "  :   "  2.0  "  ,
"  id  "  :   4  ,
"  result  "  :   {
"  resultType  "  :   "  input_required  "  ,
"  inputRequests  "  :   {
"  input  "  :   {
"  method  "  :   "  elicitation/create  "  ,
"  params  "  :   {
"  mode  "  :   "  form  "  ,
"  message  "  :   "  What email address should I send the post to?  "  ,
"  requestedSchema  "  :   {   "  type  "  :   "  object  "  ,   "  properties  "  :   {   "  email  "  :   {   "  type  "  :   "  string  "   }   }   }
}
}
},
"  requestState  "  :   "  v1.eyJwIjp7Im1ldGhvZCI6InRvb2xzL2NhbGwi…  "
}
}
```


Deserialized, the` requestState` is Mastra's record of the paused call: the tool, a fingerprint of its arguments, the question, and who asked.


requestState (deserialized)


```text
{
"  method  "  :   "  tools/call  "  ,
"  name  "  :   "  emailPost  "  ,
"  argsHash  "  :   "  I4GGwEU5Xtn4_l7aKiUxlKOEvzksi6Soa9j9xJB6TZU  "  ,
"  round  "  :   1  ,
"  suspendPayload  "  :   {   "  message  "  :   "  What email address should I send the post to?  "   },
"  principal  "  :   "  anonymous  "  ,
"  iat  "  :   1792419520
}
```


### Tasks


Tasks, previously an experimental feature, are now an official extension. A long-running tool call can respond straight away with a task handle and keep working in the background, while the client checks on it with` tasks/get` until the result is ready.


We're investigating options for supporting tasks in` @mastra/mcp` . In the meantime, a long-running tool can report progress with` context.mcp.progress()` .


src/mastra/tools/summarize-posts.ts


```text
import   {   createTool   }   from   "  @mastra/core/tools  "  ;


export   const   summarizePosts   =   createTool  ({
// ...
execute  :   async   ({ slugs }, context)   =>   {
for   (  const   [index, slug]   of   slugs.  entries  ()) {
// ...
await   context.mcp?.  progress  ?.({
progress: index   +   1  ,
total: slugs.length,
message:   `  Summarized   ${  slug  }`
});
}
return   { done:   true   };
}
});
```


### Deprecations


The spec deprecates the HTTP+SSE transport, roots, sampling, logging, and OAuth Dynamic Client Registration. The[deprecated features registry](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/deprecated) lists the migration path and earliest removal date for each.` @mastra/mcp` 2.0 has already removed the SSE transport, roots, sampling, and Dynamic Client Registration, and it only accepts clients on the 2026-07-28 spec.


## Upgrading from MCP 1.0


The[migration guide](https://mastra.ai/reference/migrations/mcp-v2) explains how to upgrade in more detail.


## Get started


Install the latest` @mastra/core` and` @mastra/mcp` :


Terminal


```text
npm   install   @mastra/core@latest   @mastra/mcp@latest
```


note


Requires` @mastra/mcp@2.0.0` and` @mastra/core@1.76.0` or later, added in[PR #23876](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/pull/23876) .


Create an` MCPServer` and pass it your tools:


src/mastra/mcp/blog-server.ts


```text
import   {   MCPServer   }   from   "  @mastra/mcp  "  ;


export   const   blogServer   =   new   MCPServer  ({
id:   "  mastra-blog  "  ,
name:   "  Mastra Blog  "  ,
version:   "  1.0.0  "  ,
description:   "  Search and read posts from the Mastra blog.  "  ,
tools: {
/* ... */
}
});
```


Register the server on your Mastra instance:


src/mastra/index.ts


```text
import   {   Mastra   }   from   "  @mastra/core  "  ;
import   {   blogServer   }   from   "  ./mcp/blog-server  "  ;


export   const   mastra   =   new   Mastra  ({
// ...
mcpServers: {   "  mastra-blog  "  : blogServer }
});
```


For more information and full configuration options, see:


